course: french
lesson: 14
title: My family
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In French the word for "my" changes with the word after it: mon père (my dad), ma mère (my mum), mes parents (my parents).
explain: Many family words come in a boy form and a girl form: un cousin and une cousine, un oncle and une tante, un fils and une fille.
explain: Picture a French kitchen: the children call their grandparents mamie and papi, like nan and grandad, while a school form says grand-mère and grand-père.
explain: Plural letters are often silent: deux frères sounds exactly like deux frère, so you can only see the s, not hear it.
explain: Some family words look funny: une belle-mère is a stepmum, not a "beautiful mum", and sœur is written with the joined letters œ.
fact: J'ai deux frères means "I have two brothers"; the plural s is written but not heard.
fact: After ne ... pas, un, une and des usually change to de: j'ai une sœur, but je n'ai pas de sœur (I don't have a sister).
fact: "My" is mon before a masculine word (mon père), ma before a feminine word (ma mère) and mes before a plural (mes parents).
fact: Before a feminine word starting with a vowel sound, ma changes to mon so it is easy to say: mon amie (my girl friend), mon école.
fact: Children call their grandmother mamie and their grandfather papi (or papy); the full words are la grand-mère and le grand-père.
fact: un cousin is a boy cousin and une cousine ("koo-ZEEN") is a girl cousin; un oncle is an uncle and une tante is an aunt.
fact: An only child is fille unique (a girl) or fils unique (a boy); enfant unique works for anyone.
fact: le fils (the son) sounds like "FEESS": the l is silent and the s is said.
fact: un demi-frère is a half-brother; une belle-mère is a stepmum or a mother-in-law, and un beau-père is a stepdad or a father-in-law.
fact: frérot ("fray-RO") and sœurette ("sur-ET") are cosy slang for little bro and little sis.
fact: œ is a special letter made of o and e joined together: sœur (sister), cœur (heart), œuf (egg).
fact: Slang here: "wee" (Scotland) means small; "bruv" means mate; "yeah nah" (Australia) means no; "my lover" (Bristol) means dear; "dead" (Ireland) means very; "no cap" means honestly; "tamping" (Wales) means fuming; "our kid" (Liverpool) means my brother or sister.
exercise: Ava writes "J'ai deux frère." What is right? | J'ai deux frères. | J'ai deux frèrs. | J'ai deux frère. | J'ai deux frèries.
exercise: Can you hear the s in deux frères? | No, it is only seen in writing | Yes, it is said like a z | Yes, it is said like an s | Only in Paris
exercise: Jake says "Je n'ai pas une sœur." What should he say? | Je n'ai pas de sœur. | Je n'ai pas une de sœur. | Je n'ai une sœur pas. | Je ai pas sœur.
exercise: Mia says "ma père". What is right? | mon père | mes père | ma pères | mon pères
exercise: Which is right for "my parents"? | mes parents | mon parents | ma parents | mes parent
exercise: Toby writes "ma amie" for his friend Lucie. What is right? | mon amie | mon ami | ma amie | mes amie
exercise: What do French children often call their grandmother? | mamie | maman | mémé-mère | ma tante
exercise: What does papi mean? | grandad | dad | uncle | little brother
exercise: Arjun's cousin Priya is a girl. What should he say? | ma cousine | mon cousin | ma cousin | mon cousine
exercise: What is une tante? | an aunt | a tent | an uncle | a niece
exercise: Lily is an only child. What should she say? | Je suis fille unique. | Je suis l'enfant seulement. | Je suis fils unique. | Je suis seule fille.
exercise: How does fils (son) sound? | "FEESS" | "FILLS" | "FEEL" | "FEE"
exercise: What does belle-mère mean in Freya's letter? | stepmum | beautiful mum | grandmother | big sister
exercise: What is un demi-frère? | a half-brother | a twin brother | a small brother | half of a brother
exercise: A French girl shouts "Arrête, frérot !" at her brother. What does frérot mean? | little bro | a good friend from school | stranger | uncle
exercise: Which word has the joined letter œ? | sœur | frère | mère | père
test: Ava says, "Wee s, big deal." ("Wee" means small.) Why does it matter? | In writing, the plural needs the s even though you can't hear it | The s changes the word into "sisters" | Without the s it means one brother and one sister | It doesn't matter, French has no plural forms at all, not even in writing
test: Jake asks, "Bruv, why's une gone?" ("Bruv" means mate.) What is the rule? | After ne ... pas, un, une and des usually become de | After ne ... pas, une always becomes la | une disappears before every family word, like sœur and frère | Jake's teacher made a mistake
test: Mia says, "Yeah nah, I thought ma was for my." ("Yeah nah" means no.) What's the fuller truth? | mon for masculine, ma for feminine, mes for plural | ma is for all words, mon is only for boys' names | ma is for parents, mon is for children | mon is old-fashioned, ma is modern
test: Toby's gran says, "But she's a girl, my lover." ("My lover" means dear.) Why is it mon amie? | ma becomes mon before a feminine word starting with a vowel sound | amie is a boy's word, so it takes mon | mon is used for every friend, boy or girl | Toby's gran is right: it should be ma amie
test: Charlotte says mamie "sounds dead cute." ("Dead" means very.) When would you use grand-mère instead? | On a school form or in a formal letter | When talking to your gran at home | When your gran is very old | Never, grand-mère is a rude word
test: Arjun says "mon cousin" about Priya. What would happen in a French class? | The teacher would say: she's a girl, so ma cousine | The teacher would say it's perfect | The teacher would say: use mon cousine | The teacher would say: use ma oncle
test: Lily says "Je suis fille unique" sounds "kinda fancy, no cap." ("No cap" means honestly.) What would a boy who is an only child say? | Je suis fils unique. | Je suis fille unique. | Je suis fils uniques. | Je suis frère unique.
test: Harvey moans, "Tamping, all them silent letters!" ("Tamping" means fuming.) What is odd about fils? | The l is silent and the s is said | Both the l and s are said | Both the l and s are silent, like in many French words | Only the f is said
test: Freya's penpal asks if her stepmum is really beautiful. Why? | belle-mère contains belle, which means beautiful | belle-mère means "mum who rings bells" | Freya wrote "une jolie mère" | The penpal knows her stepmum
test: Charlie asks his dad, "Is frérot like 'our kid'?" ("Our kid" means my brother.) Where should Charlie use it? | Chatting with friends, not in written work | In a letter to the head teacher | In his French exam answers | Only with grown-ups he doesn't know
test: Grace in Toronto asks, "Eh, is that a typo?" about œ in sœur. What's the answer? | No, œ is a real French letter made of o and e | Yes, it should be written "sur" | Yes, the o and e were glued together by mistake when typing | No, œ is an emoji
test: ★★★ Emma's mum's sister has one son and one daughter. What are they to Emma? | mon cousin et ma cousine | mon frère et ma sœur | mon oncle et ma tante | mon cousine et ma cousin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the selection among mon, ma and mes? | It is governed by the gender and number of the noun which follows | It is governed by the gender of the speaker | It is governed by the age of the person named | It is governed solely by the first letter of the speaker's name
exam: Identify the correct rendering of "I do not have a sister". | Je n'ai pas de sœur | Je n'ai pas une sœur | Je ne suis pas sœur | Je n'ai sœur pas
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the noun belle-mère. | It denotes a stepmother or a mother-in-law | It denotes a mother of remarkable beauty | It denotes a grandmother upon the paternal side | It denotes an elder sister
exam: It is given that a boy has no brothers or sisters. Determine the expression by which he describes himself. | Je suis fils unique | Je suis fille unique | Je suis frère unique | Je suis fils seul
